   "is_new": true,
   "source": {
    "label": "thm:hardness",
    "number": "Theorem 9",
    "quote": "For every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$, there exists a finite function class $\\F_n \\subseteq [0,1]^{\\A_n}$ over action set $\\A_n$ of size $2^{n+1}+1$, such that for every $\\epsilon, \\delta \\ge 0$, $\\depth_{\\epsilon, \\delta}^0(\\F_n) \\le 2$, and such that the following holds. If there exists a bandit learning algorithm for every $\\F_n$ with running time that is polynomial in $n$, then $\\mathrm{RP}=\\mathrm{NP}$."
   },
   "confidence": "medium",
   "notes": "The two-query algorithm uses brute-force SAT search (exponential time); theorem's main content is computational hardness: no poly(n)-time learner unless RP=NP, although ERM, online estimation (EST(T)=O(1)) and maximization oracles run in O(n^2)/O~(n^2). Remark 10 extends to small-variance noise and to sigma=1 with QC O~(n^2)."
  },
